WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:23.220
[Music]

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.740
This is the story of a miraculous white powder that is helping to win the war.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:35.360
Its name is dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane; for short, DDT.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:44.360
It may one day prove our greatest weapon towards victory over an enemy more murderous even than the facists.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.940
Against disease.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.040
Time was when disease conditions in other lands didn't concern us much.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.570
The Black Death, bubonic plague of India, the Orient, Africa.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.070
Periodically sweeping through the crowded millions.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.660
Transmitted from the rat to the human by--the flea.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.840
Typhus, common to nearly every densely populated city on Earth.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.500
Explosive epidemics wiping out almost entire populations.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.650
Transported by--the louse.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.040
Malaria, scourge of the tropics, carried by--the Anopheles mosquito.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:36.690
And the many filth diseases that can be borne by flies, including the dread cholera.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:41.310
But these were all in far-off places with strange names.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:48.790
And maybe, in the security of our homes and families, we shuddered at the thought that such things could happen in the 20th century.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:57.530
And then suddenly, they weren't in another world after all.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:06.580
Our own men were out there, all over the globe, and disease was as much a menace as the enemy's bullets.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.920
We learned the full import of that--at Bataan.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:18.680
[Music]

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:27.560
Of our 36,000 troops left in the last days of Bataan, 20,000 were out on their feet or down with malaria.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.800
The mosquito had helped the Jap to bring on the end.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.350
The flea, the louse, the fly could bring similar disaster wherever our men went.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:45.720
In anticipation of this, the nation's scientific resources had already been marshaled by the War Department to meet the challenge.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:56.200
Something new must be found to fight the menace of the disease-bearing insect, to take the place of insecticides no longer available because of the war.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:02.230
Through the National Research Council, this United States Department of Agriculture Laboratory at Orlando, Florida

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.470
was one of the several places in which the work was centered.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:12.950
It was the job of this laboratory to raise the healthiest, sturdiest specimens of the common disease-bearing insects,

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:16.830
and then, to find the means of killing them.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:23.580
Hundreds of insecticides were tried out here, developed from materials sent in from all parts of the world.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.790
Some were fairly effective, even good.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:35.040
Compounds and solutions that dropped a mosquito in mid-air, could stun a louse, quickly dispatch a fly or a flea.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:43.580
But something better was needed. Something that would kill all four insects and could be produced in the enormous quantities needed.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:52.400
If possible, something that might continue to kill, for a few hours anyway, or better, a few days after application.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:03.520
November 16th, 1942 was the day that brought in a new insecticide sample from Switzerland. An accompanying letter gave some of the details.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.640
One feature sounded particularly interesting, yet a little unbelievable.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:17.480
That was the claim that after a single application in a barn, this insecticide had continued to kill flies for a month thereafter.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:22.560
But scientists were accustomed to hearing exaggerated claims.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.430
The new material was given a code number and started along the testing routine.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:36.810
First, an elementary test against lice. A solution of the material is placed in a beaker and the lice are added.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:43.840
Yes, it kills, but of course you can't catch all the lice in the world and put them in beakers.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:48.590
Now, to apply it in powder form on the body of a volunteer.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.510
The lice are placed in a sleeve on the subject's arm.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:05:02.720
Another sleeve which contains the standard army delousing powder, for purposes of comparison, is removed first.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.470
The results are as good as expected, with only a few lice surviving.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.150
Now for the other sleeve, dusted with powder prepared from the new material.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.220
The results: every last louse dead.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:32.030
A fuller test: more volunteers harboring lice wear suits of underwear dusted with the new material.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.120
Results: again, every last louse dead.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:45.610
The new material, then, is already an important discovery, a better louse powder than any now in use.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:52.440
But how good is it against the other vicious three: the mosquito, the flea, the fly?

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.000
First, is it adaptable, readily solvent for use in solution form?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:06.490
Yes. Kerosene, diesel oil, even fuel in crankcase oil, dissolve it easily. That's promising.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:12.980
It's easy to get these materials in the field. An emulsion is readily prepared with both fresh and salt water.

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:19.160
It has shown really remarkable qualities for dispersion and suspension in every medium tested.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:24.450
Very valuable, if its killing properties in these agents prove out.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.400
Its action against the common pest mosquito is excellent.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:35.960
Dissolved in fuel oil, a tenth of a pound per acre is enough to kill every mosquito in the area.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:42.870
But it's the Anopheles mosquito that carries malaria. What about that?

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:49.620
A little stronger dosage is needed, but it's still amazingly potent. This is a find.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:57.050
In dust form, it has many advantages over the old standby Paris Green against the malaria-carrying Anopheles.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:07.520
The laboratory's scientists now know the material is good. Very good, that's definite.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:16.970
And now, what if...that information that came in the letter with it, about still killing weeks after the first application.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:25.170
In a testing chamber, just a box with a glass cover, a light spray of the material in five percent emulsion was applied.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:33.880
Then, the scientists waited 48 hours. At the end of that time, flies were put in the box. Big healthy flies.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:41.850
In five minutes they were in violent convulsions. In a little while they'll all be dead. Check.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:52.290
Mosquitoes next. The malaria kind. They didn't last as long as the flies and another day had gone by. The spray was a day older.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:56.580
Cockroaches the next day. Sure death.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:02.090
And six days after the original spraying, lice were put in.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:11.160
The lethal effects of the insecticide hadn't weakened an appreciable amount and this was not the end of its fantastic strength.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:18.110
Fourteen months later, one year and two months after that original spray, without a drop being added,

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:24.920
the new insecticide was still killing all comers almost as quickly as on the first day.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:37.080
Another thing, clothing impregnated with emulsion of the substance, was still killing lice after the clothing had been thoroughly laundered from six to eight times.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:45.380
Science had come on its greatest weapon against the insect killer, an incredible new weapon against disease.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:54.840
The War Department was quick to put the new substance into use in the field, under the abbreviated name DDT.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.860
But let a medical officer tell you how effective it has proved.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:09.870
[Dr. J.S. Simmons:] DDT is the most powerful of the new weapons that the Army's now using in its war on the insect-borne diseases.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:18.140
The development of this weapon holds a spectacular example of the effective ways in which all of the scientific agencies

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:24.580
of this country have cooperated with the medical departments of the armed forces.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:30.240
Because we are armed with DDT, we no longer fear typhus fever.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:40.980
This chemical affords protection to American soldiers against the mosquitoes that carry malaria, yellow fever, dengue, and filariasis.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:46.560
And against the ever-present flies that spread certain filth diseases.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:52.530
DDT is now being used by the Army in all of our tropical theaters.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:56.170
However, there is still much to be learned about it.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:05.370
Therefore our research collaborators continue their investigations in the laboratory and in the field.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:10.400
We already know of the miraculous results it has produced.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:17.470
But its full potentiality against all the insects that spread disease must be determined.

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:26.490
I consider this amazing chemical the most valuable contribution of our wartime medical research program,

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:32.410
for the future health and welfare not only this nation, but of the entire world.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:42.140
[Narrator:] This part of the world has already experienced the effectiveness of DDT in one of the miracles of modern medical history.

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:52.220
This was Naples, Italy shortly after the Allied occupation. Its crowded population lacked almost everything for the safeguarding of public health:

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:56.970
food, fuel, blankets, housing, clothing, sanitation.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:05.390
And refugees from all parts of Italy were swarming in. With them, the louse, the perfect set-up for epidemic.

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:06.860
And then it struck: typhus.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.160
The Naples area was immediately placed off limit to Allied military personnel.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:20.220
Within armies, typhus has won and lost more wars than all the strategy and weapons ever developed.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:28.920
[Music]

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:33.000
But for the jammed civilian population of Naples, there seemed little hope.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:41.770
Winter was only arriving when typhus hit Naples, and never in history had a typhus epidemic ended before the warmth of the spring.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:50.690
Then, DDT was brought in. Dusting with DDT was begun at once.

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:58.640
First, every case of typhus was tracked down and every person who had been exposed was rid of the murderous louse by the powder.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:05.680
Next, the 40,000 Italians dwelling in jam-packed air raid shelters were deloused.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:19.960
[Music]

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:28.490
Finally, 43 DDT stations were set up throughout the city and the entire remaining population was dusted.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:35.250
In January, when the large-scale dusting started, 60 new cases of typhus a day were being reported.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:40.900
Another month and it might well have been 600 or 6,000 cases a day.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:45.490
Yet, by the middle of February, the menace of the epidemic was over.

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:55.650
DDT had won, by not only removing the lice when dusted, but by continuing to kill long after application, preventing re-infestation.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:03.740
In the Pacific, DDT goes to war against malaria in a big way, by airplane.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:12.010
On D-Day at Morotai, we hit both enemies at the same time. The Jap and disease.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:20.890
Simultaneous with the bombing of our two-legged enemy, we went after the mosquito with DDT spray, and as a result of the spray

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:27.560
malaria was already under control before our troops set foot on the island.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:37.420
The Japanese did not live long enough to enjoy the island rid of mosquitoes by DDT.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:48.870
Yes, today DDT is necessarily a military weapon. We're turning it out by the thousands of tons, but every ounce is needed for the war effort.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:54.950
When the fascist is finally beaten though, DDT will be available to all.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:14:00.650
Already is has been found to be highly effective against many kinds of agricultural insect pests.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:07.820
It will aid in stamping out disease around the world.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:14.420
[Music]

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:21.740
And the homecoming of the healthiest army in military history will be in no small part due to DDT,

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:26.440
our great new weapon for war today, and peace tomorrow.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:34.600
[Music]

